# .env.example — copy to .env and fill in real values (never commit .env)

# ── Anthropic / LLM ──────────────────────────────────────────────
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# Per-agent local model routing (sensitive projects) is configured per project, in the
# project's own settings - local_fast_model, local_fast_url, local_deep_model, local_deep_url -
# not here. See api/models.py ProjectSettings.

# ── Admin credentials (required — app will not start without these) ──
ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-in-production

# ── JWT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
JWT_SECRET=change-me-in-production

# ── Home organisation ────────────────────────────────────────────
# The consultancy. One organisation, not one per client: every engagement belongs to it, and
# an org_admin appointed in it reaches every engagement. Seeded automatically on first system
# connection, and the organisation new projects are registered to when the creator's token
# carries no org_id of its own (which is every sysadmin). Renaming it afterwards through
# PATCH /auth/orgs/{id} sticks - the seed keys on the slug.
HOME_ORG_SLUG=future-edge
HOME_ORG_NAME=Future Edge Consulting

# ── ChromaDB ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CHROMA_HOST=localhost
CHROMA_PORT=8002
# For ChromaDB Cloud — set CHROMA_API_KEY to use CloudClient instead of HttpClient
CHROMA_API_KEY=
CHROMA_TENANT=default_tenant
CHROMA_DATABASE=default_database

# ── Storage ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DATABASE_DIR=./data
# Content-addressed caches (e.g. synthesised speech) live under DATA_DIR/<cache-name>
DATA_DIR=data
PROJECTS_DIR=./projects

# ── URLs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Production: set to your public domain, e.g. https://taskreimagination.ai
# PUBLIC_URL=https://taskreimagination.ai
#
# This is the BOOTSTRAP value, not the last word. A sysadmin can set the public URL in the
# admin area, and once one is stored it WINS over this variable - so editing here and
# restarting changes nothing on a deployment that has ever saved one, which is the symptom
# to expect rather than a broken .env. "Revert to environment default" on the admin
# dashboard gives it back (DELETE /admin/platform-settings). It still matters: it is
# what a fresh deployment sends links from before anybody opens that page, so a new
# deployment left at localhost:3000 mails out dead login and interview links.

# ── Email (Resend) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
FROM_EMAIL=TaskReimagination <noreply@taskreimagination.ai>
# Used entire for platform mail (the welcome email). For a project's mail only the DOMAIN
# is taken from here: those messages are sent from a role address minted from the
# correspondent's agent id - stakeholder-manager@ and pam@ - so that a reply reaches
# whoever holds the role rather than a named person. One mailbox per role, and moving
# domain here moves them all. See api/services/outbound_mail.py.
#
# Where a project with dev_mode on sends its mail instead of to its real stakeholders.
# dev_mode defaults to on for every new project, so set this to an address you own
# before enabling Resend - held mail is delivered, not discarded.
DEV_MODE_ADDRESS=Patrick@FutureEdge.consulting

# The signing secret for Resend's inbound webhook, from the webhook's page in the Resend
# dashboard. `whsec_` followed by base64.
#
# Leave it unset and POST /api/inbound-mail/resend refuses everything: that endpoint is
# unauthenticated, public, and writes a participant's words into a project database, so an
# absent secret has to close the door rather than open it. Nothing arrives until
# taskreimagination.ai is a verified domain in Resend with inbound routing configured
# anyway - see api/services/inbound_mail.py.
RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...

# ── Voice / Transcription ─────────────────────────────────────────
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=your-elevenlabs-api-key
DEEPGRAM_API_KEY=your-deepgram-api-key

# Where a system-level alert goes - the deployment's operator, meaning whoever holds the
# provider keys, pays the bills and restarts a server. Deliberately NOT the client's governance:
# PAM's daily report resolves its recipients to stakeholders carrying is_reviewer / is_approver
# / is_governor, and "your transcription provider is out of credit" is not a governor's
# business. Two alerts use it today:
#
#   - An interview on an engagement that is not granted hosted inference could not reach
#     Deepgram, so it was stopped rather than handed to the browser's own recogniser (which
#     streams the participant's audio to Google or Apple). See api/services/speech_policy.py.
#   - The vector store could not be reached by something that needed it - an interview's answers
#     going unindexed, a document failing to ingest, or a crew agent's retrieval coming back
#     empty mid-run. See api/services/health_checks.py. Any of those is silent today: indexing
#     deliberately never fails an interview, because SQLite is the system of record, so without
#     this the only trace is a log line nobody is tailing.
#
#   - A paid provider refused a call - Anthropic, Deepgram, ElevenLabs or Chroma Cloud. See
#     api/services/provider_health.py. The alert names **which of three things to do**, because
#     they are opposite actions: out of credit means top up, a 429 means back off and topping up
#     changes nothing, and a malformed request (Deepgram's "Keyterm limit exceeded") is a code
#     defect where both topping up and waiting are wasted. An operator told the wrong one of
#     these spends money and still has the fault.
#
# Two provider quirks are worth knowing, because a status-code reading gets both backwards:
# Anthropic answers an exhausted balance with 400 (not 402), and ElevenLabs answers an exhausted
# quota with 401 - the same status it uses for a bad key.
#
# Leading indicators are captured only where they are free. Anthropic's rate-limit headers are
# read off responses already being made; nothing is polled. Deepgram's balance endpoint needs an
# Admin key and this one is a Member, ElevenLabs' usage read needs a re-scoped key, and Chroma
# Cloud publishes no quota endpoint at all - so for those three the refusal at the call site is
# the whole signal.
#
# Alerts are rate-limited to three per hour per incident. The incident is the **store**, not the
# project - a cloud outage is one incident across every engagement using the cloud, so keying
# per project would multiply it by the number of live projects. It is not one global key either:
# CHROMA_API_KEY plus a project's CLOUD_VECTOR_STORE grant decide whether that engagement uses
# Chroma Cloud or a local server, so a deployment can have two vector stores failing
# independently and one must not silence the other.
#
# Platform mail, so `dev_mode` - which defaults to true - cannot redirect an outage alert to the
# operator, where its absence would read as "no outage". It sends from FROM_EMAIL entire.
#
# Leave it blank and no message is sent: ADMIN_USERNAME is a login rather than an address and
# DEV_MODE_ADDRESS is where *held* mail goes, so neither honestly means "the system
# administrator" and guessing would be worse than an absence. Both alerts are written to the log
# at ERROR either way. The speech one additionally records against the interview session, which
# the interview sessions panel shows to the consultant running the engagement; the vector store
# one has no such surface, so with this blank the log is its only trace.
# ADMIN_ALERT_EMAIL=alerts@example.com

# ── Web search ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
TAVILY_API_KEY=tvly-...

# ── Cloudflare Tunnel (production only) ──────────────────────────
# CLOUDFLARE_TUNNEL_TOKEN=your-tunnel-token
